.TH FIXPERMS 1 "October 2026" "fixperms 1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fixperms \- repair owner, mode and ACL of files created from an elevated Cygwin shell

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fixperms
[\-n] [\-v] [\-d] [\-u USER] [\-g GROUP] [\-w] [\-x] [\-f] [\-r FILE]... [\-R] [\-P] [\-I]
.I path
\&...
.br
.B fixperms
[\-r FILE]... [\-R]
.B \-\-list\-rules

.SH DESCRIPTION
Files and directories created from an elevated ("Run as administrator") Cygwin shell \- a
.B pip install
into
.IR /usr/local ,
a build in a project directory, anything under
.I \(ti/.local
\- often come out owned by the Administrators group, with Windows ACLs that a normal, non-elevated
session cannot read or write: "Permission denied" on
.BR ls ,
failed imports, and a trailing
.B +
in
.BR "ls \-l" .
.B chmod
alone does not fix them, because the trouble is in ACL entries it leaves in place: named user and group
entries (such as
.B user:chris:\-\-\-
or
.BR group:Administrators:rwx ),
a mask, NULL SID and deny ACEs, and default (inheritable) entries such as
.B default:user::\-\-\-
that hand the same damage on to every new file.

.B fixperms
walks each
.I path
top-down, without following symbolic links, and for every file and directory:
.IP 1. 4
sets the owner and group to the invoking user (or
.BR \-\-user / \-\-group )
with
.BR chown ;
.IP 2. 4
replaces the whole ACL with a minimal one using
.BR "setfacl \-s" :
directories get
.BR u::rwx,g::r\-x,o::r\-x ,
plus the same as default entries so that new files and subdirectories inherit a clean ACL;
files get
.BR u::rw\-,g::r\-\-,o::r\-\- ,
or
.B rwxr\-xr\-x
if they are executable (see below). Cygwin turns this into a short, canonical Windows ACL: the owner,
the group and Everyone, plus CREATOR OWNER, CREATOR GROUP and Everyone entries that are inherited by new
files. Setuid, setgid and sticky bits are kept;
.IP 3. 4
if
.B setfacl
cannot set the ACL (a file system Cygwin cannot map POSIX ACLs onto, for example), resets the Windows ACL
to the inherited one with
.B "icacls /reset"
and then sets the mode with
.BR chmod .
.PP
That is the default. Places that need other modes or another owner \-
.IR \(ti/.ssh ,
credential files,
.IR /var/mail ,
.IR /tmp ,
other people's home directories \- are handled by rules; see
.B RULES
below.
.PP
Entries whose owner, group and ACL are already right are left alone, so a second run changes nothing.
Directories are fixed before they are listed, so a directory the user cannot even open is fixed first and
then descended into.

.SS Inheritance
On Cygwin, a real run (not
.BR \-n )
fixes each subtree that needs nothing but the defaults in one go, by Windows inheritance, which is about
ten times faster than one entry at a time and leaves a cleaner result:
.IP 1. 4
.B "chown \-R \-h"
sets the owner and group of the whole subtree;
.IP 2. 4
the subtree's top directory gets its owner, group and Everyone entries plus inheritable ones, with
separate entries for files and for directories, so that files inherit
.B rw\-r\-\-r\-\-
and directories
.BR rwxr\-xr\-x ;
.IP 3. 4
.B "icacls DIR\e* /reset /T"
replaces every ACL below it with the inherited one;
.IP 4. 4
executables (see below) get
.BR "chmod 755" ,
and setuid, setgid and sticky bits are put back.
.PP
Everything else below the directory then carries only inherited entries: Cygwin shows them as a plain mode,
with no
.BR + ,
and what anyone creates there later, from Cygwin or from Windows programs, inherits the same. A file that
Cygwin creates there comes out 644, a directory 755.
.PP
.B icacls /T
follows junctions and directory symbolic links out of the tree, and under a Cygwin mount point it sees the
Windows directory rather than what is mounted there. So before using inheritance on a directory,
.B fixperms
looks through it with one
.BR find ,
and a directory with a link to a directory, a junction, an unreadable directory, another file system or a
Cygwin mount point anywhere below it is done entry by entry instead, as is any directory that a rule could
match or have below it (home directories,
.IR /var/mail ,
.IR /tmp ).
Its subdirectories are looked at in turn, so only the directories on the way to the trouble go slowly.
.PP
With
.BR \-v ,
a subtree done this way is one line,
.RB \(dq "reset by inheritance: DIR (N entries below it, M made executable)" \(dq,
rather than a line per entry; use
.B \-n
or
.B \-I
to see each entry.
.B \-I
turns inheritance off. It is also off with
.B \-w
when the group is the user's own (as Cygwin sets up a Microsoft account's group), since an inherited group
entry would then merge into the owner's.

.SS Executable files
A file is made executable for everyone if any of its execute bits was set, or if it starts with
.B #!
(a script),
.B MZ
(a Windows .exe, .dll or Python .pyd, which Cygwin will not load without execute permission) or
.B \e177ELF
\- an "a+rX"-style rule, never a blanket
.BR +x .
With
.BR \-\-exec\-by\-content ,
existing execute bits are ignored and only the content decides; use it when a tree's execute bits cannot be
trusted, as with files created under Windows-default ACLs, which often show
.B rwx
on everything. A file that cannot be read until it is fixed is checked after its ACL has been set.

.SS Ownership and elevation
Changing a file's owner to yourself from a non-elevated shell works only if you already own it or the ACL
grants you "take ownership"; files owned by Administrators usually need an elevated shell. When a
.B chown
fails like that,
.B fixperms
says so, carries on with the rest, and at the end prints the command to run again from an elevated Cygwin
terminal. It never touches the ACL of a file whose owner it could not change.
.B fixperms
counts a shell as elevated when
.B "id \-G"
includes the Administrators group (544), which Cygwin lists only for an elevated token.

.SS Safety
.B fixperms
refuses, without
.BR \-\-force ,
to work on
.IR / ,
the Cygwin root (as
.IR / ,
.I D:/cygwin
or
.IR /cygdrive/d/cygwin ),
a drive root, or a top-level directory such as
.I /usr
or
.IR C:/Users .
It never follows a symbolic link (a link's own owner is changed with
.BR "chown \-h" ,
nothing else), and does not descend into a directory on another file system. Paths may contain spaces and
any other characters.

.SH RULES
Each entry's absolute path is matched against a table of rules; the first rule that matches decides the
entry's mode and owner, and entries that no rule matches get the defaults above. Rules come from, in order:
each
.B \-\-rules
file, the user rules file
.I \(ti/.config/fixperms/rules
(or
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fixperms/rules ),
and the built-in table.
.B \-\-list\-rules
prints the table in force;
.B \-\-no\-rules
drops the user rules file and the built-in table. With
.BR \-v ,
each change says which rule it came from.
.PP
A rule is one line of four fields; # starts a comment:
.RS
.nf
PATTERN   DIR-MODE   FILE-MODE   OWNER
.fi
.RE
.TP
.I PATTERN
An absolute path glob.
.B *
and
.B ?
match within one path component,
.B **
matches across components, and a leading
.B \(ti
stands for any home directory
.RI ( /home/NAME ,
and the target user's home if it is elsewhere). On Cygwin the match ignores case, and paths are matched in
Cygwin's own spelling, so
.I D:/cygwin/var/mail
is
.IR /var/mail .
.TP
.IR DIR-MODE ", " FILE-MODE
The mode for a directory or for anything else that matches.
.B \-
means the default (755 or 644);
.B =
keeps the current mode, minus write for group and others (with the group's rights read through the ACL
mask, and the group never given less than others), while the owner always gets rw, or rwx on a directory,
and a file with executable content gets x where it has r: it never loosens anything. Or an octal mode such
as 700, 600 or 1777: a file under a rule like this is made executable only by its content (#!, MZ, ELF),
which adds x where the mode has r (600 becomes 700), not by old x bits. Setuid, setgid and sticky bits in a
rule are added to those the entry already has. A world-writable directory gets default ACL entries without
write for group and others, so what is created in it is not world-writable.
.TP
.I OWNER
.B user
\- the invoking user, or
.BR \-\-user / \-\-group .
.B keep
\- leave owner and group alone.
.B home
\- the account the
.I /home/NAME
directory is named after (the invoking user in their own home, or when
.B \-\-user
is given).
.B base
\- the account named by the entry's own name, as in
.IR /var/mail/NAME .
Any other word names an account. When
.B home
or
.B base
finds no such account, owner and group are left alone.
.PP
The built-in rules:
.RS
.nf
\(ti/.ssh                       700   \-     home
\(ti/.ssh/**.pub                700   644   home
\(ti/.ssh/known_hosts*          700   644   home
\(ti/.ssh/**                    700   600   home
\(ti/.gnupg, \(ti/.getmail         700   \-     home
\(ti/.gnupg/**, \(ti/.getmail/**   700   600   home
\(ti/.netrc \(ti/.authinfo* \(ti/.git\-credentials \(ti/.msmtprc \(ti/.fetchmailrc
\(ti/.pgpass \(ti/.my.cnf \(ti/.Xauthority \(ti/.aws/credentials \(ti/.kube/config
\(ti/.docker/config.json        \-     600   home
\(ti                            =     \-     home
\(ti/**                         =     =     home
/var/mail, /var/spool/mail   1777  \-     keep
/var/mail/*, /var/spool/mail/*   \-  600   base
/tmp, /var/tmp, /var/log     1777  \-     keep
/tmp/**, /var/tmp/**, /var/log/**  =  =  keep
/var/empty                   755   \-     keep
/etc/ssh_host_*_key, /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key        \-  600  keep
/etc/ssh_host_*_key.pub, /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub \-  644  keep
/etc/sshd_config, /etc/ssh/sshd_config               \-  644  keep
.fi
.RE
.PP
So home directories are never loosened (a private
.I mbox
stays 600), each one goes to the account it belongs to, OpenSSH's permission checks are satisfied, mailboxes
stay private to their own users, and shared and service directories keep their owners. A user rules file
might add:
.RS
.nf
/srv/www/**        775   664   www
\(ti/bin/**           \-     \-     home
.fi
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be changed, and predict which changes would need an elevated shell; change nothing.
Implies
.BR \-v .
A directory that cannot be read until it is fixed is reported as not looked into.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
List every change, with the old and new owner, group and mode. Give it twice to list unchanged entries too.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-no\-recurse
Fix only the named paths, not what is under them.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-user USER
Owner to set. Default: the invoking user.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-group GROUP
Group to set, by name or number. Default: the primary group of the invoking user (or of
.BR \-\-user ).
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-group\-write
Give the group write access: 775 and 664 instead of 755 and 644 where no rule gives a mode, and under an
.B =
rule (as in home directories) wherever the group can already read. Without it, write access for the group
and for others is removed.
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-exec\-by\-content
Ignore existing execute bits; set them only on files that start with
.BR #! ,
.B MZ
or ELF magic.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Allow
.IR / ,
the Cygwin root, drive roots and top-level directories.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-rules FILE
Read rules from
.IR FILE ,
before the user rules file and the built-in ones. May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-no\-rules
Ignore the built-in rules and the user rules file
.RB ( \-\-rules
files still apply).
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list\-rules
Print the rules in force, first match first, and exit.
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-no\-progress
Don't show the progress line (see
.BR OUTPUT ).
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-no\-inherit
Fix every entry one at a time; don't reset whole subtrees by inheritance (see
.BR Inheritance ).
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the version and exit.

.SH OUTPUT
While it works, if standard error is a terminal,
.B fixperms
keeps one line there up to date: a spinner, how many entries it has checked and fixed so far, and
the path it is on (shortened from the left to fit). The total is not known in advance \- a
directory is only listed once it has been fixed \- so there is no percentage. The line is cleared
before anything else is printed, and at the end;
.B \-P
turns it off.

Each change (with
.B \-v
or
.BR \-n )
is one line; a
.B +
after the old mode means the old ACL had extended entries that were removed:
.RS
.nf
fix site-packages/pyte: mode dr-xrwxr-x+ -> 755 (ACL cleaned)
fix site-packages/pyte/__init__.py: owner Administrators -> chris; mode -r--rw-r--+ -> 644 (ACL cleaned)
.fi
.RE
Then a summary, and on standard error each path that could not be fixed, with the reason:
.RS
.nf
fixperms: 412 checked, 3 already fine, 409 fixed (409 owner/group, 409 mode/ACL), 2 symlinks not followed
.fi
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
See what an elevated pip install did, then fix it from an elevated Cygwin terminal:
.RS
.nf
.B fixperms \-n /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages
.B fixperms \-v /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages
.fi
.RE

Fix a project directory, leaving it group-writable:
.RS
.B fixperms \-w \(ti/github/myproject
.RE

Give another user's home back to them, from an elevated shell (see first what would change):
.RS
.nf
.B fixperms \-n /home/miche
.B fixperms /home/miche
.fi
.RE

Fix just one directory entry, not its contents:
.RS
.B fixperms \-d /usr/local/bin
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything is (or, with
.BR \-n ,
would be) fixed.
.TP
.B 1
Something could not be fixed (or would need an elevated shell), a path does not exist, or a path was
refused.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, a bad rule, or a required tool
.RB ( stat ,
.BR chown ,
.BR chmod ,
.BR getfacl ,
.BR setfacl ,
.BR realpath )
is missing.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/fixperms/rules
The user rules file
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fixperms/rules
if that is set), read before the built-in rules. The home is the target user's.

.SH NOTES
Written for Cygwin, where
.B getfacl
and
.B setfacl
map POSIX ACLs onto Windows ACLs; it also works on Linux file systems with ACL support, where "elevated"
means root.

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

